    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d311">

                <pb facs="0370" n="1204" xml:id="pg1204"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 658.119/180a</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State
                        to the Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Page</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1918-01-24T18:00:00-05:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">January 24, 1918, 6 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>6345. For Sheldon [from
                    War Trade Board]:</p>
                <p>No. 35. Your No. 50. Terms of proposal to Sweden noted. If this proposal is
                    accepted we desire to maintain the principle already agreed upon of equal
                    division of neutral tonnage between United States and Great Britain. The <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Österland</hi> and <hi rendition="#italic">Jemtland</hi>
                    would be excepted from the operation of this principle as the disposition of
                    these ships is specifically dealt with by proposals. We are, however, willing
                    that the United States’ share of tonnage be contributed to make up the 100,000
                    tons to be devoted temporarily to the needs of France and Italy and to save time
                    now agree to leave in England’s hands the allocation of our share of this
                    tonnage to France and Italy—for not exceeding period of 60 days. Cable how it is
                    divided, giving names of ships.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Polk</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
